          "id": "10505",
          "body": "**⚠️ Review risk: this was built against a link↔rep cardinality that is still contradictory across three calls.**\n\n* **Jul 28, Joyce:** all links sit at deal level, CMS picks from a dropdown — implicitly **one-to-many**.\n\n* **Aug 4, Emily \\[14:45\\]:** \"Typically like a rep for a deal wouldn't have more than one link… it would be managed on Salesforce\" — then \\[16:12\\]: \"they can, in their lifetime, have, like, one-to-one. there's a potential for them to switch to a different link on the same deal.\"\n\n* **Aug 4, Joyce \\[15:43\\]:** \"I think what you're saying is, like, we just do one-to-one\" — after acknowledging she changed the mock-up at the offsite without discussion.\n\n* But Emily also confirmed there **will** be rep sharing / multiple reps on one link — which contradicts one-to-one.\n\nThe attached Deal mockup shows a Links related list with a **Reps count column** (4, 2, 0) and a **Default** flag — i.e. many reps per link, with one default. That is a third model again.\n\n**Before accepting this review, pin down:** (a) can one link serve multiple reps simultaneously? (b) can one rep hold multiple active links on the same deal? (c) what does the Default flag actually govern?\n\nAlso noted Jul 28: there is **no Salesforce-side guardrail** that a link belongs to only one rep — that has to be enforced in the CMS. If (a) is \"no\", we are relying on a system we do not control to protect our data model. Worth pushing back on.",
          "created": "2026-08-10T13:10:31.382-0400",
          "updated": "2026-08-10T13:10:31.382-0400",
          "jsdPublic": true
        },
        {
          "id": "10506",
          "body": "Closing this as having been built. Will create addtiional tickets to update the fields and naming conventions as the CMS <> SF Integration details are solidified.",
